{% note
   text="Processes show what is currently running on a Linux system.\n\nDuring enumeration, unusual processes can reveal privileged applications, unexpected services, scripts, or activity associated with persistence or post-exploitation." /%}

### Basic Enumeration

{% terminal
   title="process enumeration"
   commands=[{cmd: "ps aux"}, {cmd: "ps -ef"}, {cmd: "top"}, {cmd: "pgrep -a -u root"}] /%}

{% finding
   severity="informational"
   text="Look for:\n\n• Unknown processes\n• Processes running as root unexpectedly\n• Executables from unusual directories\n• Scripts running in the background\n• Suspicious command-line arguments\n• Processes associated with unknown services" /%}

{% terminal
   title="inspect a process"
   commands=[{cmd: "ps -fp <PID>"}, {cmd: "readlink -f /proc/<PID>/exe"}, {cmd: "tr '\\0' ' ' < /proc/<PID>/cmdline"}] /%}

{% warning
   text="A process list is only a snapshot.\n\nShort-lived processes may disappear before they can be observed, so process enumeration works best when combined with logs, service configuration, and filesystem evidence." /%}

{% tip
   text="When you find an interesting process, identify the executable behind it and inspect its location, ownership, permissions, and parent process." /%}

### Takeaway

Process enumeration gives you a snapshot of active execution. Focus on unusual processes and follow them back to the files, services, or users responsible for starting them.
